{"t":1795224666,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"2ea5e30c-873f-4156-8eb8-1b53fc1f3380","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-11-20T20:27:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 202027\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n327 PM EST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nGuidance have trended a little faster lifting the system\noffshore of the Southeast U.S. coast Sunday.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Coastal low pressure to bring light to moderate rain \n  Sunday.\n\n- 2) Conditions turn cooler next week, with a coastal low \n     forecast to track up the East Coast later in the week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Coastal low pressure to bring light to moderate rain\nSunday.\n\nThe low pressure center offshore of the Southeast U.S. coast is\nexpected to track a little quicker now on Sunday, while\nremaining just offshore of the mid Atlantic coast. While rain is\nexpected everywhere in our CWA Sunday, amounts seem to have \ntrended lower with areas east of I-95 expected to receive half \ninch of rain with any inch or greater totals remaining east of \nthe Chesapeake Bay. The rain may persist into Sunday evening \nacross southern MD before exiting the area by midnight Sunday \nnight. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Conditions turn cooler next week, with a \ncoastal low forecast to track up the East Coast later in the \nweek.\n\nSunday's system will depart off toward the northeast on Monday. \nWinds will shift to out of the northwest, but cold advection will be \nminimal with the primary trough axis still off to our northwest over \nthe Great Lakes. Temperatures are forecast to climb into the 60s to \nnear 70 beneath a mix of sun and clouds.\n\nA stronger surge of cold advection will move through Monday night. \nCooler conditions are expected on Tuesday as a result, with highs \nforecast to be in the upper 40s to mid 50s (upper 30s to mid 40s \nmountains). Conditions will remain dry and winds will shift to out \nof the north as high pressure builds over the Great Lakes. \n\nHigh pressure will progress eastward toward New England on \nWednesday. Meanwhile, a fast moving upper trough will translate \neastward from the center of the CONUS toward the Appalachians. An \narea of low pressure will likely develop downstream of this trough \nalong the Southeast US coast on Wednesday. This system will then \ntrack northward along the East Coast Wednesday night into Thursday. \nThis far out, both deterministic and ensemble guidance show a wide \nrange of potential solutions, so it's far too early to get into \nspecific details. However, at least some precipitation appears \nlikely. Current ensemble guidance suggests that precipitation would \nmost likely fall as rain at lower elevations, with greater chances \nfor wintry precipitation at higher elevations, especially across \nwestern portions of the forecast area. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /20Z FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\n\nRain is expected to begin late Saturday night into Sunday\nmorning with MVFR conditions developing Sunday morning. Winds\nare expected to remain below 10 kt under stable conditions. \n\nVFR conditions are expected on both Monday and Tuesday. Winds will \nbe out of the northwest on Monday, before shifting to out of the \nnorth on Tuesday.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\n\nWinds will begin strengthening Saturday night into Sunday with\nSCA conditions likely. \n\nSmall Craft Advisory conditions appear possible over the waters at \ntimes in northwesterly flow on Monday, and then again in northerly \nflow Monday night into Tuesday.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...LFR/BRO/KJP\nAVIATION...LFR/BRO/KJP\nMARINE...LFR/BRO/KJP\n"},"forecast":{"t":1795224593,"days":{"2026-11-20":{"hi":null,"lo":42,"pop":0,"short":"Partly Cloudy"},"2026-11-21":{"hi":